import json
from pathlib import Path
from fractions import Fraction as F
p=Path('experiments/break-it-down-dag/2026-10-24-graph-first/runs/g08')
g=json.loads((p/'dag.json').read_text()); nodes={n['id']:n for n in g['nodes']}
c='https://probabilitycourse.com/chapter1/1_4_0_conditional_probability.php';t='https://probabilitycourse.com/chapter1/1_4_2_total_probability.php';b='https://scikit-learn.org/stable/modules/naive_bayes.html';m='https://developers.google.com/machine-learning/crash-course/classification/accuracy-precision-recall'
def ev(u,l,cl):return dict(url=u,locator=l,claim=cl,access='Relevant source section opened 2026-10-24; also supplied in verified packet')
def change(i,a,e=None):
 nodes[i]['answer']=a;nodes[i]['status']='supported'
 if e:nodes[i]['evidence']=e
change('counts','In a constructed population of 10,000 messages, 1% gives 100 spam and 9,900 legitimate messages. The rates yield 100 × 0.90 = 90 spam flags and 9,900 × 0.10 = 990 legitimate flags. These are model-expected counts, not a measured inbox.',[ev(c,'Chain rule, Equation 1.5','Multiply class share by its within-class flag rate to obtain the joint share.'),{'url':'request.txt','locator':'User-provided rates and prevalence','claim':'Inputs are 90%, 10%, and 1%; counts are original arithmetic checked in calculations.json.'}])
change('posterior','Among 90 + 990 = 1,080 flagged messages, 90 are spam: 90/1,080 = 1/12, or about 8.33%. The denominator is all flags. Most flags come from legitimate mail because there are 99 times as many legitimate messages initially. A randomly selected flagged message therefore has an 8.33% spam chance under the given rates; the flag raises the starting 1% chance but does not make spam likely.')
change('basechange','Let p be the initial spam share written as a decimal. Holding the 90% detection and 10% false-positive rates fixed, the spam share among flags is (0.90 × p)/[(0.90 × p) + (0.10 × (1 − p))]. At starting shares 0.1%, 1%, 10%, and 50%, the answers are about 0.893%, 8.33%, 50%, and 90%. As the spam population grows, spam contributes more of the flags relative to legitimate mail. At 0% and 100% starting spam the answers are respectively 0% and 100%. These comparisons assume the within-class flagging rates remain unchanged.',[ev(t,'Two-event partition formula','Sum weighted spam and legitimate contributions in the denominator.'),ev(c,'Conditional-probability definition','Divide the spam-and-flag probability by the overall flag probability.'),{'url':'calculations.json','locator':'base_rates','claim':'Original exact-rational calculations with fixed rates; includes boundaries.'}])
change('words','A feature is a measured property, such as whether the word “offer” occurs. Its likelihood within spam is the fraction of spam messages containing it; compare that with its fraction within legitimate mail. Treat its presence as the flag and use the same calculation. Hypothetically, if its frequencies were 90% and 10%, observing it at a 1% spam base rate would again give 8.33%. These invented word frequencies illustrate the rule; no actual word statistics are claimed.')
change('learn','Labeled examples identify which messages are spam or legitimate. Class counts estimate the prior. For a presence feature, count messages containing the word within each class; for a word-count model, count word occurrences against total word occurrences in that class. Small added counts, called smoothing, prevent an unseen training word from forcing a zero estimate. Bernoulli models use presence and absence; multinomial models use occurrence counts.')
change('independence','Naive Bayes assumes that, after fixing spam or legitimate, learning the other feature values does not change a feature’s chance. It then replaces the second clue’s chance given both class and first clue with its chance given class alone, and multiplies. Real words may co-occur, so this simplification can count related evidence too strongly.')
change('scores','For each class, multiply its prior by the modeled chance of the complete observed features within that class. Divide the spam score by the sum of both scores to normalize; compare scores to choose the more likely class. In an explicitly hypothetical two-feature presence model, if each clue occurs in 90% of spam and 10% of legitimate mail and the clues are independent within each class, observing both gives scores 0.01 × 0.90 × 0.90 = 0.0081 and 0.99 × 0.10 × 0.10 = 0.0099, yielding 45% spam. If the clues always occur together, the second adds no information and the correct answer stays 8.33%. Bernoulli models also use absent features; a full message score is not generally a product over present words alone.')
nodes['scores']['evidence'].append(ev(c,'Equation 1.7','The true second-clue probability may depend on the first clue; perfectly duplicate clues add no information.'))
nodes['scores']['evidence'].append({'url':'calculations.json','locator':'two_clue_example','claim':'Original arithmetic for explicitly hypothetical independent and duplicate clues.'})
change('target','At 1% spam prevalence, 90 real spam flags are outnumbered by 990 legitimate flags per 10,000 modeled messages, so only 8.33% of flags are spam. Detection among spam and spam among flags use different denominators. A larger spam base rate raises the latter share when the flagging rates stay fixed. Words work as evidence through their within-class frequencies; naive Bayes combines their modeled likelihoods with the class prior using conditional independence. Its normalized score need not be an empirically reliable confidence.',[ev(c,'Conditional-probability definition and Equation 1.5','Distinguish reverse conditional questions and compute joint shares.'),ev(t,'Two-event partition formula','Both classes contribute flags.'),ev(b,'1.9 Bayes equations and probability-output warning','Word evidence combines with priors under a conditional-independence model; scores may be poorly calibrated.'),{'url':'calculations.json','locator':'given_case and base_rates','claim':'Original computations instantiate the general formulas with user-provided rates.'}])
joint=dict(id='joint',question='How is the chance of two clues together found before assuming independence?',answer='Within spam, first count the fraction with clue one. Among that subset, count the fraction also having clue two. Multiply those fractions to obtain the spam share having both clues. Repeat within legitimate mail. The second fraction can differ from its frequency in the whole class.',status='supported',evidence=[ev(c,'Chain rule, Equation 1.7','Within a class, the joint clue probability equals the first-clue probability times the second-clue probability conditioned on class and first clue.')])
g['nodes'].insert(next(i for i,n in enumerate(g['nodes']) if n['id']=='independence'),joint)
g['edges']=[e for e in g['edges'] if not(e['from']=='words' and e['to']=='independence')]
g['edges'] += [dict(zip(['from','to','why'],x)) for x in [('words','joint','The combined event must first be defined by individual observed clues.'),('conditional','joint','The second clue is counted in the subgroup already selected by class and first clue.'),('joint','independence','Independence simplifies the general combination rule by dropping the dependency on previous clues.'),('posterior','scores','Normalizing class scores applies the same reverse-conditional calculation used for flags.')]]
for n in g['nodes']:
 for e in n['evidence']:
  if e.get('access')=='supplied verified source packet':e['access']='Verified source packet and directly opened relevant section, 2026-10-24'
calc={'given_case':{'messages':10000,'spam':100,'legitimate':9900,'spam_flags':90,'legitimate_flags':990,'all_flags':1080,'spam_fraction_among_flags':str(F(90,1080))},'base_rates':[]}
for prior in [F(0),F(1,1000),F(1,100),F(1,10),F(1,2),F(1)]:
 post=F(9,10)*prior/(F(9,10)*prior+F(1,10)*(1-prior))
 calc['base_rates'].append({'prior_fraction':str(prior),'posterior_fraction':str(post),'posterior_percent':float(100*post)})
a=F(1,100)*F(9,10)**2;d=F(99,100)*F(1,10)**2
assert a/(a+d)==F(45,100)
calc['two_clue_example']={'independent_spam_score':str(a),'independent_legitimate_score':str(d),'independent_posterior':str(a/(a+d)),'duplicate_posterior':str(F(90,1080)),'type':'Constructed inputs; not measured word statistics'}
(p/'calculations.json').write_text(json.dumps(calc,indent=2)+'\n')
g['revisions'].append({'round':1,'stage':'source_verification_and_gap_resolution','gaps':['Exact count and base-rate arithmetic','Why multiplying marginal word likelihoods needs a separate assumption','Feature estimation and absence handling'],'searches':[],'opened_sources':[{'url':c,'locators':['Definition','Equation 1.5','Equation 1.7']},{'url':t,'locators':['Two-event partition formula']},{'url':b,'locators':['1.9','1.9.2','1.9.4']},{'url':m,'locators':['Recall','False positive rate','Precision']}],'evidence':['Read relevant primary source sections directly.','Exact fractions computed in calculations.json.'],'changes':['Resolved numeric and synthesis gaps.','Added joint prerequisite after reading general conditional chain rule, replacing words→independence with words→joint→independence and conditional→joint.','Added posterior→scores: normalization reuses the reversed-conditional calculation.','Specified word probability denominators, smoothing, absence handling, hypothetical numbers, and independence limitations.'],'remaining_gaps':[]})
g['completion']={'status':'complete_graph_only','remaining_required_gaps':[],'limits':['No empirical inbox or word data supplied.','Constant flagging rates assumed during prevalence comparisons.','Model calibration requires empirical assessment; none performed.','Shared renderer responsible for visual output; no report or rendered-view checks performed here.']}
s=json.dumps(g,indent=2,ensure_ascii=False)+'\n';(p/'dag.json').write_text(s);(p/'history'/'round-1-dag.json').write_text(s)
print('Final graph saved.')
